<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José María Chacón</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José María Chacón was a Spanish naval officer and the last Spanish governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, appointed in the 1780s. He oversaw the settlement promoted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cedula-of-population">Cedula of Population</a>, which multiplied the island's population. In February 1797, when a British expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-abercromby">Sir Ralph Abercromby</a> and Admiral Henry Harvey arrived, the Spanish squadron was scuttled and Chacón surrendered the island, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> formally ceded in 1802.</p></div>
